1
00:00:01,853 --> 00:00:06,273
Upgrading React 17 to 18 looks like a version bump.

2
00:00:07,041 --> 00:00:14,910
In a real codebase nobody knows where it's used, what will break, or whether it actually worked. So it gets put off. Again.

3
00:00:14,813 --> 00:00:23,285
Codebase Doctor maps every usage, plans the change, waits for your approval — and opens a PR only when your own checks pass.

4
00:00:23,339 --> 00:00:26,899
It runs inside IBM Bob. You ask in plain English.

5
00:00:26,969 --> 00:00:32,927
Our custom migration-doctor skill activates on its own and drives 12 tools on our MCP server.

6
00:00:34,045 --> 00:00:40,990
Attach the official upgrade guide as a PDF — Bob analyses the repo, plans, and records your approval.

7
00:00:41,605 --> 00:00:48,264
The approved plan becomes a live to-do list in Bob, and each step is a real MCP tool call.

8
00:00:49,805 --> 00:00:54,432
And we built it with Bob: the codemods, the migration rules, and the tests behind them.

9
00:00:54,947 --> 00:00:58,206
Here's a real recorded run, replayed call by call.

10
00:01:00,043 --> 00:01:05,820
First, it maps every React usage: 11 files, 19 API calls.

11
00:01:05,890 --> 00:01:09,195
And it catches a peer conflict before anything is installed.

12
00:01:09,047 --> 00:01:12,797
The rules are checked against the guide: 8 breaking changes apply.

13
00:01:12,867 --> 00:01:15,703
Every file gets a risk score from real evidence.

14
00:01:16,676 --> 00:01:21,946
Then a 10-step plan: 4 automated, 6 for a human. And it is NOT approved.

15
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:28,618
If the agent tries to skip ahead, the server refuses.

16
00:01:29,695 --> 00:01:31,364
What about a casual reply?

17
00:01:33,285 --> 00:01:38,900
Refused. Nothing changed. Only the exact word “approved” unlocks the branch.

18
00:01:40,139 --> 00:01:48,233
Now it works: a real npm install, and codemods for createRoot, hydrateRoot and act — each in its own commit.

19
00:01:48,303 --> 00:01:52,990
Steps with no safe automation are flagged as manual. Honestly.

20
00:01:58,527 --> 00:02:01,882
Then it runs the repo's own lint, tests and build.

21
00:02:01,952 --> 00:02:06,711
The first run fails — for real. So the pull request stays blocked.

22
00:02:10,426 --> 00:02:17,505
The fixes are made in Bob, guided by that diagnosis, and recorded step by step. Second run: everything passes.

23
00:02:24,216 --> 00:02:28,898
Only now does the pull request open — with the full report as its description.

24
00:02:33,522 --> 00:02:39,992
One report shows it all: the risk, every change, both verification runs, and what still needs a human.

25
00:02:42,257 --> 00:02:46,061
Codebase Doctor. Every upgrade deserves a diagnosis.

26
00:02:46,131 --> 00:02:47,577
Built with IBM Bob.

